<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need a Lot of Books! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need a Lot of Books!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: communication_1: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. communication_2: Relay complex information from teachers to students.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a discussion on a group project, using simple questions and visuals (e.g., holds up a checklist and says, 'What's next on the list?'). Can relay complex teacher instructions by breaking them into simple parts with visuals (e.g., writes 'sort books' and points to the correct shelf). Can guide classmates in completing project tasks with written steps and gestures (e.g., writing 'return books' and gestures putting books on a shelf).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a short, simple video clip (1 minute) of a person trying to find a book in a library, looking confused. Then, the teacher asks: "What did the person want? Where was she? Did she look happy?" Students respond. The teacher then says: "Today, we are going to help people like her understand the library! We will work on our library video project."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to facilitate a discussion or relay instructions. The teacher holds up a simplified "Library Video Project Checklist" (e.g., "1. Plan scenes," "2. Write script," "3. Film," "4. Edit"). The teacher points to "1. Plan scenes" and says, "First, we need to plan our scenes for the video. What scenes should we have? For example, someone checking out a book." The teacher then points to the next item and asks, "What's next on our list?" The teacher emphasizes clear articulation and uses gestures. The teacher models the intonation for yes/no questions: "Can we film in the library?" (emphasizing 'library'). The teacher also models short and long vowel sounds with words like 'pick' and 'find'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is our main goal today? (To plan our library video.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Go to the catalog,' is that clear? (Yes.) What should you do? (Go to the catalog.)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(5-7 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a simple, step-by-step guide on "How to Borrow a Book" using visuals. This guide includes a simplified version of the library's actual process. The guide will include: "1. Search for a book in the catalog. 2. Find the book on the shelf. 3. Take the book to the librarian. 4. Check out the book." The teacher also shows a simple diagram of a library section (e.g., 'Fiction Section', 'Non-fiction Section', 'Children's Books'). The teacher reviews key vocabulary (catalog, shelf, librarian, check out, return, fiction, non-fiction) and models polite requests with modals: "Can I borrow this book?" and "You should be quiet in the library."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short set of illustrated instructions (e.g., a "Library User's Mini-Guide") and a visual diagram of library sections. Register: Instructional/Informative. Format: Illustrated steps and simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Use library-related vocabulary in peer discussions. Pragmatic: Asking for and giving information about resources: 'Can I borrow this book?'. Pragmatic: Making polite requests: 'You should be quiet in the library.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students work in pairs to answer simple questions based on the guide: "What is the first step to borrow a book?" "Where can you find a book about animals?" (pointing to non-fiction section). "Can you talk loudly in the library?" (Expected answer: "No, you should be quiet.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10-12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</b> Students practice relaying and simplifying information.
                <b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Past simple tense (e.g., 'I read a good book last week.'), Modals can/should (e.g., 'Can I borrow this book?', 'You should be quiet in the library.'), Imperatives (e.g., 'Go to the library catalog.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> library, book, catalog, author, title, section, librarian, check out, search, find, select, return, interesting, quiet, helpful, many, all, most.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice intonation for yes/no questions using modals (e.g., 'Can I take out this book?'), identifying short (/ɪ/ 'pick') and long (/aɪ/ 'find') vowel sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Simplifying Instructions):</b> The teacher provides a slightly complex sentence (e.g., "Patrons must consult the digital catalog to ascertain the availability of desired literary resources."). Students work in pairs to rephrase it using simpler words and target grammar, aiming for imperatives or simple modal usage (e.g., "You should search the catalog to find books." or "Search the catalog for books."). They practice with 3-4 sentences. Teacher monitors pronunciation of words like 'pick' and 'find'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Relaying Teacher's Instructions):</b> The teacher gives a "complex" instruction related to the project (e.g., "For our video, you need to depict the process of returning a book and ensure proper shelf placement according to the Dewey Decimal System."). In small groups, students must relay this instruction to a new 'team member' (another student) using simpler language, gestures, and perhaps a quick drawing on the board. One student mediates, others listen and confirm. They might say, "We should show how to return books. You should put them back on the correct shelf."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Facilitating a Mini-Discussion):</b> Students form groups of three. One student acts as a "facilitator" for a specific small task from the "Library Instructional Video" project (e.g., "What part of the library should we film first?"). The facilitator uses simple questions like "What do you think is best?" or "What's next?" and visuals (e.g., a sketch of the library layout) to guide the discussion. They practice asking "Can we film here?" with correct intonation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "In the Library".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Group Project Planning & Mediation</b>
                Students work in their project groups to develop the initial *outline* and *plan* for their "Library Instructional Video." Each group is given a section of the library process to focus on (e.g., "How to Find a Book," "How to Check Out," "Library Rules"). Within each group, one student is designated as the "Project Facilitator" for a specific task. The facilitator's role is to mediate the discussion, relay instructions, and guide classmates. They must use simple questions, visuals (e.g., drawing on a whiteboard, showing a picture), and gestures to make sure everyone understands and participates. For example, a facilitator might say, "Okay, our part is 'How to Check Out.' What should we show first? Can we use a real book?" (intonation practice). They should also use imperatives like "Go to the librarian" or modals like "You should have your library card ready." The group then orally presents their mediated plan for their section of the video to another group, ensuring all steps are clear and simple to understand for the audience of the instructional video. They should use vocabulary like 'author', 'title', 'summary', 'page', 'chapter', 'research'. They should mention what they liked (e.g., 'I liked the story, it was interesting.') from a book they read last week for their research.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "I Need a Lot of Books!" and Scenario: "In the Library".</b> The video directly addresses how to navigate and use the library to find and manage books.
                <b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining and mediating, collaborative interaction to build the video plan, and communicative task completion (creating a clear, shared plan for their video section).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy: Peer and Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a checklist to observe how well students (especially the designated facilitators) mediate discussions and relay information. Peers also assess their facilitator using a simplified checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents a 1-minute "mini-pitch" of their video section, with the facilitator explaining the main steps and a key library rule (e.g., "You should be quiet in the library."). The facilitator demonstrates how they would explain one complex instruction from their video plan in simple terms, using visuals or gestures, to help their classmates understand. For example, "We need to highlight important information. You should find the main ideas." (gesturing to highlight words).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of mediated explanation (Are the instructions simple and easy to follow?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'catalog', 'shelf', 'librarian', 'check out', 'return').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modal verbs ('can', 'should') and imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of visuals and gestures to support understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially for yes/no questions and short/long vowels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active facilitation of group discussion (asking questions, encouraging participation).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing I explained clearly to my group today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I still need to practice to help my classmates understand better?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did mediating the project plan help me understand the library process better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our 'Library Instructional Video' do I think will be most helpful for others, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in helping students mediate and simplify information for the project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further develop their mediation skills in future projects, especially regarding specific grammar points like past simple or a wider range of vocabulary?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Need a Lot of Books!" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, likely introducing new vocabulary and scenarios, building on the communicative skills developed in this unit. Students will apply their understanding of library processes and communication skills to future tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2-3 minutes)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>